Every C program needs a `main` function as its entry point — the compiler looks for it first when your program runs, which is exactly why you got that linker error. [1](https://cs50.harvard.edu/x/2025/notes/1/)

A minimal skeleton looks like this:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{

}
```

If you want to go deeper on program structure, toggle **Study Assist** mode. Now, looking at your empty `hello.c` — what do you think should go *inside* the curly braces to print `hello, world`?

